    "id": "madas/alevel-applied-q/q6",
    "question": "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-q/q6-question-1.svg\" alt=\"The records in a dentist’s surgery show that 15% of the patients that make an appointment fail to turn up. a) In a day with 15 appointments determine the probability that … i. … exactly 2 patients will fail to turn up. ( ) 2 ii. … at least 2 patients will fail to turn up. ( ) 2 b) Use a distributional approximation to find the probability that in a month with 400 appointments, more than 45 but at most 65 patients will fail to turn up. ( ) 6 The surgery administrator feels that the percentage of patients that make an appointment and fail to turn up is likely to change in the future. The surgery tries an automated system of generating message reminders on patients’ mobile phones.\" style=\"--madas-width:585.3px\"></figure><figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-q/q6-question-2.svg\" alt=\"It is required to find whether the proportion of patients that fail to turn up is different from 15% , by monitoring the next 20 appointments. c) Determine the critical region for this test, at the 10% level of significance. ( ) 5 d) Write down the critical region for the same test if the probability of rejecting at either tail is as close as possible to 5%. ( ) 1\" style=\"--madas-width:589.3px\"></figure>",
    "steps": [
      "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-q/q6-solution-1.svg\" alt=\"Assume independent appointments: (a) For X ∼B(15, 0.15), 15 0.1520.8513 ≈0.2856 , [M1] [A1] (i) P(X = 2) = 2 (ii) P(X ≥2) = 1 −P(X ≤1) ≈0.6814 . [M1] [A1]\" style=\"--madas-width:677.3px\"></figure>",
      "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-q/q6-solution-2.svg\" alt=\"(b) For 400 appointments use Y ∼N(60, 51). [M1] [A1] The binomial parameters give np = 400(0.15) = 60, np(1 −p) = 400(0.15)(0.85) = 51. √ √ The endpoint scores are 5.5/ 51 and −14.5/ 51. The continuity-corrected interval is 45.5 &lt; Y &lt; 65.5, [M1] [A1] giving 65.5 −60  45.5 −60  Φ √ −Φ √ ≈0.7582 . [M1] [A1] 51 51 (c) Test H0 : p = 0.15 [B1] against H1 : p̸ = 0.15. Under the null, X ∼B(20, 0.15). [A1] In the lower tail, P(X = 0) = 0.03876 [A1] and P(X ≤1) ≈0.17556. In the upper tail, P(X ≥6) ≈0.06731 and P(X ≥7) ≈0.02194. [A1] With a 5% cap per tail, the region is X = 0 or X ≥7 . [A1]\" style=\"--madas-width:677.3px\"></figure>",
      "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-q/q6-solution-3.svg\" alt=\"(d) The probabilities closest to 0.05 are 0.03876 and 0.06731, so the region is X = 0 or X ≥6 . [A1]\" style=\"--madas-width:677.3px\"></figure>",
      "<p class=\"madas-total\">Total mark for question 6 is 16 marks.</p>"
    ],
    "promptText": "The records in a dentist’s surgery show that 15% of the patients that make an appointment fail to turn up. a) In a day with 15 appointments determine the probability that … i. … exactly 2 patients will fail to turn up. ( ) 2 ii. … at least 2 patients will fail to turn up. ( ) 2 b) Use a distributional approximation to find the probability that in a month with 400 appointments, more than 45 but at most 65 patients will fail to turn up. ( ) 6 The surgery administrator feels that the percentage of patients that make an appointment and fail to turn up is likely to change in the future. The surgery tries an automated system of generating message reminders on patients’ mobile phones. It is required to find whether the proportion of patients that fail to turn up is different from 15% , by monitoring the next 20 appointments. c) Determine the critical region for this test, at the 10% level of significance. ( ) 5 d) Write down the critical region for the same test if the probability of rejecting at either tail is as close as possible to 5%. ( ) 1"
